# Commercial S8.1 health diagnosis and recovery

## Safe state after the first deploy

- S7 remained active and healthy.
- S8 was stopped and disabled by the fail-closed controller.
- The Application checkout returned to `935518614d4e9e6ce302c75bf81d6e5ca2a4f1d4`.
- The verified pre-deploy backup remained available.
- Adult media, AVS, payment, publishing and the production Adult workflow remained closed.

## Instrumentation defect

The first S8 health wrapper collapsed every subprocess, JSON, Telegram, database,
polling and product-boundary failure into `S8_PUBLIC_TELEGRAM_HEALTH_RED`. This
prevented an exact privacy-safe diagnosis and made bounded Telegram calls appear
like an indefinite wait.

## Server-proven root cause 1

The S8 notification runtime locks and materializes a due broadcast and therefore
executes `UPDATE commercial_s8_broadcasts`. Migration 0022 granted the runtime
role only `SELECT` on that table. A PostgreSQL 17 acceptance using the actual
`tu1nz_adult_commercial_s3_runtime` role reproduces the missing capability. A
new reversible migration grants only the missing table-level `UPDATE` privilege.
The server probe reproduced this exact condition as
`S8_NOTIFIER_BROADCAST_UPDATE_PRIVILEGE_MISSING`, SQLSTATE `42501`; all other
nine probe components were green. After migration 0023, all ten prestart
components were green.

## Server-proven root cause 2

The first complete runtime cycle then failed before readiness with SQLSTATE
`42P18` (`IndeterminateDatatype`). An empty Telegram poll calls
`heartbeat(None, ...)`. Its SQL used the same untyped null placeholder in an
`IS NULL` condition, so PostgreSQL could not infer a type. Prestart probes did
not exercise this runtime-only empty-poll heartbeat.

The controller waited for readiness, then aborted exactly as designed: S8 was
stopped, the Application checkout returned to the S7 release, and S7 remained
active with zero restarts. The minimal recovery is to bind all nullable update
ID placeholders explicitly as `BIGINT` and add a real runtime-role PostgreSQL
acceptance for the empty-poll heartbeat. No schema or privilege expansion is
required for this second fix.

## Server-proven activation timing defect

The complete internal DE/EN acceptance, duplicate join, leave/rejoin, clean S8
stop/start and database-backed kill-switch proof all passed. Public SFW
activation then stopped and started S7 to load the already-installed landing
binding, but evaluated the local health probe immediately after `systemctl
start`. The process was active with zero restarts and emitted
`S7_PUBLIC_RUNTIME_READY` moments later; both local and external health were
green after the bounded startup interval. The immediate probe therefore
produced a deterministic readiness race rather than an S7 runtime failure.

The controller remains fail-closed and now uses a bounded 30 x 2-second S7
readiness wait after each controlled S7 start in activation and rollback paths.
Activation also gives the external landing/deep-link health contract the same
bounded convergence window. It still uses explicit stop then start and never
`restart`. A timeout remains a hard failure and preserves the existing rollback
boundary.

## Server-proven rollback start-limit defect

The bounded activation retry proved that S7 reached `S7_PUBLIC_RUNTIME_READY`
after the controlled activation start and then remained healthy for 73 seconds.
The activation gate nevertheless timed out and entered the fail-closed rollback.
During that rollback, systemd rejected the next S7 start with
`start-limit-hit`: the preceding S8.1 acceptance, kill-switch, activation and
rollback sequence had exhausted the unit's existing start-rate allowance.

This is not an S7 application crash. The last S7 process exited cleanly with
status 0 when the rollback stopped it, and the subsequent start was never
launched. The fail-closed result is S8 inactive and disabled, the Application
checkout restored to `935518614d4e9e6ce302c75bf81d6e5ca2a4f1d4`, Control at
`7d9a5bc5ca5b8d4352b05de92e0a491a567100c9`, and S7 failed solely with
`start-limit-hit`; the public landing consequently returns HTTP 502.

Because an S7 disruption is an explicit hard stop, no automatic start recovery
was attempted. The next separately authorized recovery must first preserve the
current evidence, then clear only the S7 unit's failed/rate-limit state and
perform exactly one S7 start followed by bounded local and external health
checks. The controller must be corrected and tested so that every intentional
S7 stop/start transition accounts for systemd rate limiting before any further
S8 activation attempt.

The controlled recovery completed successfully with S7 healthy internally and
externally, zero restarts, and S8 still inactive and disabled. Its post-check
then found that the root-owned backup process had refreshed only the Control
repository index as `root:chatops` mode `0600`. The checkout commit and tree
were correct, but `chatops` could no longer run `git status`. The backup now
streams bundles produced by Git running as `chatops` into the root-only backup
directory and checks both repository index owners and modes before and after
every backup. Recovery of the existing index is limited to restoring the known
`chatops:chatops` owner and mode `0660`; no runtime service transition is part
of that rights repair.

The first rights finalization proved that root-owned `git bundle verify` also
refreshes the checkout index, so changing only bundle creation was insufficient.
Verification now opens each root-only bundle as a root file descriptor and
passes that already-open descriptor to Git running as `chatops`. This keeps the
backup directory mode `0700` and bundle mode `0600`, gives no persistent file
access to another identity, and prevents Git from recreating the working index
as root. The post-backup owner/mode guard remains mandatory and fail-closed.

The server execution proved that `runuser` closes the inherited descriptor, so
that design correctly failed closed but could not finish verification. The
final design never runs root Git inside a working checkout: bundle creation and
all provenance reads run as `chatops`, while each root-only bundle is verified
inside a short-lived empty bare repository under `/run`. The isolated repository
contains no application data beyond transient Git verification metadata and is
removed immediately after the check. Consequently root Git has no path to a
working index, while bundles and their enclosing backup directory remain
root-only throughout.

## Recovery controls

- Every component emits status, timestamps, elapsed time, safe reason, safe
  exception type, errno/SQLSTATE when available and retryability.
- Telegram identity, webhook and command checks are bounded to six seconds and
  run concurrently within one probe process.
- Prestart uses rollback-only waitlist, analytics and kill-switch transactions.
- Runtime readiness is emitted only after one complete polling, database,
  notifier and queue cycle.
- S8 uses inactive -> start -> health -> stop on failure. No restart is used.
- S7 is not restarted during diagnosis or S8 recovery staging.
- Public activation and rollback wait at most 60 seconds for S7 readiness after
  an explicit clean stop/start.
